Certainly! To force a text field in Adobe Acrobat JavaScript to display columns, you can use the `setColumn` method of the `TextField` object. Here's an example of how you might do this:

```javascript
// Assuming 'myTextField' is the name of your text field
var myTextField = this.getField("myTextField");

// Set the number of columns to 2
myTextField.setColumn(2);
```

This script sets the text field to display its content in two columns. You can change the number `2` to any other positive integer to display more or fewer columns. Keep in mind that the text field must be a multiline text field to use the `setColumn` method. If it's a single-line text field, you won't be able to set columns directly. In that case, you might need to use a different approach, such as splitting the text into multiple lines and then displaying them in separate text fields.

