class UserController extends Controller
{
    /**
     * Retrieve a user by ID and return as JSON.
     *
     * @param  int  $id
     * @return \Illuminate\Http\JsonResponse
     */
    public function getUserById($id)
    {
        // Find the user by ID
        $user = User::find($id);

        // Check if the user exists
        if ($user) {
            // Return the user data as JSON
            return response()->json($user);
        } else {
            // Return a JSON response indicating the user was not found
            return response()->json(['error' => 'User not found'], 404);
        }
    }
}
```

To use this function, you would typically define a route in your `web.php` or `api.php` routes file that points to this controller method, like so:

```php
Route::get('/user/{id}', 'UserController@getUserById');
```

This route will allow you to make a GET request to `/user/{id}` to retrieve a user by their ID. The `{id}` part of the URL is a route parameter that will be passed to the `getUserById` method as the `$id` argument.

