        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="icon-container">
        <input type="text" id="myInput" oninput="inputFunction()">
        <svg id="readingGlassIcon" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24" onclick="inputFunction()">
            <path d="M12 2C6.48 2 2 6.48 2 12s4.48 10 10 10 10-4.48 10-10S17.52 2 12 2zm0 18c-4.41 0-8-3.59-8-8s3.59-8 8-8 8 3.59 8 8-3.59 8-8 8z"/>
        </svg>
    </div>

    <script>
        function inputFunction() {
            // Your input function logic here
            console.log("Input function called");
        }
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

In this example:
- An input field and a reading glass SVG icon are placed inside a `div` with the class `icon-container`.
- The SVG icon is positioned absolutely to the right of the input field.
- The `oninput` event of the input field and the `onclick` event of the SVG icon both call the `inputFunction()` function.
- The `inputFunction()` function is defined in the script to perform any action you want when the icon is clicked or when the input value changes.

You can customize the `inputFunction()` to perform any specific action you need.

